cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Moderation Bias: LLM Content Moderation Benchmark"
message: "If you use this software, dataset, or findings, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Kandel"
    given-names: "Jacob"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0008-8858-6072"
repository-code: "https://github.com/jacobkandel/llm-content-moderation-analysis"
url: "https://moderationbias.com"
abstract: >
  An open-source benchmark for auditing content moderation bias across 27 Large Language
  Models spanning 16 harm categories and 2,012 evaluated prompts (2,293 in corpus).
  Biweekly automated audits track policy drift over time. Statistical methods include
  McNemar's test with Bonferroni correction, Fleiss' Kappa (inter-model agreement),
  Cramér's V (categorical effect size), Cohen's d (verbosity analysis), Wilson Score
  confidence intervals, and minimum detectable effect size (MDES) power analysis.
  Key finding: model identity (Cramér's V = 0.59) is a substantially stronger predictor
  of refusal behavior than prompt topic (V = 0.21), suggesting provider policy
  idiosyncrasies rather than rational content-based moderation.
keywords:
  - "llm"
  - "content-moderation"
  - "bias-analysis"
  - "ai-safety"
  - "benchmark"
  - "red-teaming"
  - "censorship"
  - "inter-annotator-agreement"
  - "statistical-nlp"
